data_protein_dna_complex
#
# A MIXED complex: one protein chain and one DNA chain in one structure --
# the case `ui.geometry._best_anchor_atom` exists to get right, since the
# anchor has to be chosen per RESIDUE (C-alpha here, phosphorus there)
# rather than once per file.
#
# Hand-written, not folded, because every number in it is chosen to make a
# wrong anchor choice produce a DIFFERENT ANSWER rather than the same one:
#
#   * Within a residue, no two candidate anchors share a position OR a
#     B-factor. Anchoring on the wrong atom therefore moves the trace AND
#     recolours it, instead of being invisible (which is what would happen
#     if, say, every atom in a residue carried the residue's one pLDDT).
#
#   * Chain A residue 4 is SEP -- phosphoserine, a real modified amino acid
#     -- so it contains an atom literally named "P" as well as a C-alpha.
#     It is protein and must anchor on CA (11.400, 0.000, 0.000; B 97.00).
#     Code that tried P before CA would put that residue at
#     (11.400, 4.000, 0.000) with B 20.00: 4 A away and two ramp bands down.
#
#   * Chain B residue 1 is a 5'-terminal nucleotide with NO phosphate (a
#     5'-OH end, which is what most experimental entries and some
#     predictors produce). It must fall back to C1' and stay in the trace;
#     code that only knew about P would silently drop it and shorten the
#     strand to 3 residues.
#
#   * Chain B residues 2-4 carry both P and C1', and their C1' B-factors
#     (41-43, the "<50" orange band) sit in a different ramp band from
#     their P B-factors (62-64, the "50-70" yellow band). Anchoring a
#     nucleotide on the wrong one of its own two atoms is therefore visible
#     as a colour change, not just as a sub-angstrom wobble.
#
# The two chains are 23 A apart at their nearest ends and sit in clearly
# separated pLDDT bands (protein 91-97, the ">90" blue band; DNA 61-64, the
# "50-70" yellow band), so per-chain geometry and per-chain colour are both
# testable by position alone.
#
